  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King was untouchable. Eight strikeouts, nothing past second base, a shutout line that should have been enough. It wasn't.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres left six runners on base and never scored. Three critical chances in the first two innings. Runners in scoring position three times before the third inning even started. And nothing came of any of them. This is not a one-game problem anymore. They have now stranded runners in scoring position repeatedly in the same stretch of the season where the pitching keeps holding tight. King's dominant start exposed exactly what's broken: the offense is not just quiet. It's absent when it matters most. Adrian Morejon and Mason Miller combined for two scoreless innings after, but by then the game was already written. One run on the board in Pittsburgh was enough because the Padres never found a way to answer. This is what happens when a shutout feels inevitable instead of unlucky.",
    "what_to_watch": "Bubba Chandler has a 4.38 ERA, but tonight he will not face a lineup that capitalized on a chance. The Padres are 5-5 in their last ten games. Close margins are starting to feel like a trap they cannot escape.",
